186166562 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $F(x) = \\cos(2x)$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\pi$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x \\in [0,\\pi)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x+\\pi) = f(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n\nII. $f(x) = F(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n\nIII. $f\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = f(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166562 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5888d4ce-bc45-540b-afa0-901008abfcde" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166562 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f$ is the $\\pi$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have  \n\n- $f(x+\\pi)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R},$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in[0,\\pi).$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\pi$-periodicity:\n\n$$\nf(x+\\pi)=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that $F$ itself is $\\pi$-periodic, because for any $x\\in\\mathbb{R},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x+\\pi )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(x+\\pi ))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+2\\pi )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=F(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $F$ already satisfies the periodicity rule, the $\\pi$-periodic extension agrees with $F$ everywhere, so $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=f(0)=F(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\neq f(x)$ for $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166563 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21788" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166563 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\langle f(x),g(x) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(x) g(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [0,2\\pi)$ defined above, compute $\\displaystyle \\langle \\sin(6x), \\cos(4x) \\rangle$ and determine if functions $f(x)=\\sin(6x)$ and $g(x)=\\cos(4x)$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166563 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "052f11a0-2f27-5285-83af-6d8fdb729024" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166563 #edb/ref 1074 "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\sin(6x)\\cos(4x)\n=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\big(\\sin(10x)+\\sin(2x)\\big).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{20}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x)-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{20}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(20\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{20}(1-1)-\\frac{1}{4}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\sin(6x)$ and $g(x)=\\cos(4x)$ \n$\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166564 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21789" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166564 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $F(x) = 2x^2+5$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi$-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [0,2\\pi)$ to $x\\in \\mathbb R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\left(a_n \\cos(nx) + b_n \\sin(nx)\\right).\n$$\n\nDetermine the integral expression for the coefficient $a_4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166564 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d2127a8e-14bb-5574-a542-852333fe4cca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166564 #edb/ref 1074 "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [0,2\\pi)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),g(x) \\rangle = \\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(x)g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi$-periodic function $f(x)$ that is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2\\pi),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\left( a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere $a_0,$ $a_n,$ and $b_n$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\cos(4x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1, \\sin(x), \\cos(x), \\sin(2x), \\cos(2x), \\ldots$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\na_4 \\left\\langle \\cos(4x), \\cos(4x) \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={a}_{4}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩} \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(2{x}^{2}+5)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166962 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166962 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166962 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166962 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166962 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166962 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166962 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166962 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186315068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166962 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186315069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166962 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166962 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166962 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166962 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186554574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166962 #edb/ref 1039 "Understanding the Periodic Extension of a Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166962 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186166562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166962 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "544f5154-5213-5898-a45d-4546bc8004c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166963 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166963 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166963 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166963 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166963 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166963 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166963 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166963 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166963 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166963 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314994 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166963 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166963 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166963 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186315084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166963 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166963 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166963 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186526085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166963 #edb/ref 1039 "Checking Orthogonality of Sines and Cosines" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166963 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186166563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166963 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "fadf4f64-3569-56b2-98e1-f6681892054a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166964 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166964 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166964 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166964 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166964 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166964 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166964 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166964 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166964 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166964 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186315082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166964 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166964 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166964 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166964 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166964 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166964 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining Expressions For Fourier Coefficients" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166964 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186166564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166964 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "304c015d-cbb6-5989-a102-d6f0f5f38604" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166990 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166990 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166990 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166990 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166990 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166990 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166990 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166990 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166990 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166990 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166990 #edb/ref 1045 "Introduction to Fourier Series" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166990 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186167943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166990 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166990 #edb/ref 1050 1080.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166990 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "a585794a-f4e3-5afb-9bdf-f3bee0610206" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167264 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167264 #edb/ref 1069 "q-338488" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167264 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167264 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $F(x)=2x$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $3$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,3)$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x+6)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n\nII. $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n\nIII. $f(x+2)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167264 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a8290119-5783-5de1-8f8a-4d7cbbad292c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167264 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f$ is the $3$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have \n\n- $f(x+3)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,3).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+6)=f((x+3)+3)=f(x+3)=f(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=4.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4)=f(1+3)=f(1)=F(1)=2(1)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(4)=2(4)=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)\\ne F(x)$ for $x=4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=2.$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2+2)=f(4)=f(1+3)=f(1)=F(1)=2(1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2)=F(2)=2(2)=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x+2)\\ne f(2)$ for $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167274 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167274 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167274 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340366" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167274 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167274 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [0,2\\pi ]$ defined above, select the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),1⟩=$ \n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ and $g(x)=1$ \n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167274 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "efd96307-00da-56b8-bab1-563e900c1e1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167274 #edb/ref 1074 "Evaluating the inner product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),1⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\pi )-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ and $g(x)=1$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167291 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167291 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167291 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167291 #edb/ref 1069 "q-341306" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167291 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167291 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}+9x$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${a}_{0}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167291 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5082df1a-d560-5493-a305-43900a17419a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167291 #edb/ref 1074 "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that's piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),1⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),1⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),1⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,1⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨1,1⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[x]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),1⟩ & =\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ {a}_{0} & =\\frac{2⟨f(x),1⟩}{⟨1,1⟩} \\\\ {a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{2}+9x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167943 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167943 #edb/ref 1061 600573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167943 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "822d573c-a967-52e4-854c-d2b5f14b0fff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167943 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167944 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167944 #edb/ref 1061 600574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167944 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c6f0ea8f-2008-5f49-84d9-7341157766fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167944 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167945 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167945 #edb/ref 1061 600578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167945 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f80997bd-d9ed-5a35-b485-c34868575480" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167945 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167946 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167946 #edb/ref 1061 600579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167946 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b4a0ba44-f514-5920-a47f-39a8f107bb7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167946 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167947 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167947 #edb/ref 1061 600583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167947 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "769e9784-6852-5663-879d-f2dd13159e0a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167947 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167948 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167948 #edb/ref 1061 600584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167948 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "52c506ad-0e1c-5f71-b513-284e3650dd10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167948 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167949 #edb/ref 1061 600587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167949 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4fc54380-13dd-5632-a907-a555a9ed5733" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167949 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167955 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167955 #edb/ref 1061 606168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167955 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c98bd153-6ef6-5953-a668-095e59197be9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167955 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167956 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167956 #edb/ref 1061 606214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167956 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a0bab88b-45ac-5613-b8cf-64b1eafabee1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167956 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168034 #edb/ref 1064 "In this lesson, we will represent a $2\\pi$-periodic function $f(x)$ as an infinite sum of simple sine and cosine waves. This representation is called the **Fourier series** for $f(x)$:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\frac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\big)\n$$\n\nThe key idea is that we can \"build\" a complicated periodic graph by adding together basic waves. For example, below we have a discontinuous periodic function approximated by the first few terms of its Fourier series.\n\n![](images/39/392a8df0fab00010e76036aa0fec2c60f25b5766b3cacc7983b7443d909866eb.png)\n\nThis gives us a powerful way to analyze periodic behavior by breaking it down into simple oscillations. Fourier series play a central role in modeling vibrations, signals, and other repeating patterns.\n\nTo work with Fourier series, we typically start with a function defined on a single base interval, such as $[-\\pi,\\pi),$ and then extend it to be periodic over all real numbers. This process creates what is called a **periodic extension**.\n\nIn the next slide, we will define this concept formally and look at some examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168034 #edb/ref 1065 14624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168034 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1d19e02d-99c0-5649-b7a3-bdb37c8e97e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168034 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168035 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that we are considering a $2\\pi$-periodic function $f(x)$ that is piecewise continuous on $[-\\pi,\\pi).$ We can represent $f(x)$ by its Fourier series:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\big).\n$$\n\nOur goal is to find the formulas for the coefficients $a_0, a_n,$ and $b_n.$\n\nTo do this, we'll use a powerful idea from linear algebra. We define an *inner product* for functions on the interval $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ as\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x), g(x)\\rangle=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWith this inner product, the set of functions $\\{1, \\cos(nx), \\sin(nx)\\}$ for $n\\ge 1$ is an *orthogonal set*.\n\nThis means we can think of the Fourier coefficients as *projections* of the function $f(x)$ onto each of these orthogonal \"directions\". The coefficients $a_0,a_n,b_n$ simply tell us \"how much\" of $f(x)$ lies in each of these directions.\n\nOn the next slide, we will use this idea to derive the formula for the first coefficient, $a_0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168035 #edb/ref 1065 14628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168035 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "304c879d-2d08-5f1e-985b-938b67e64dfb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168035 #edb/ref 1067 "Fourier Series Form For 2pi-Periodic Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168036 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that we can define an inner product on functions over $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ given by\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x), g(x)\\rangle=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nAlso recall that functions $f$ and $g$ are called *orthogonal* (with respect to this inner product) if $\\langle f(x), g(x)\\rangle=0.$\n\nThe key fact we will use throughout this lesson is that the functions\n\n$$\n1,\\ \\cos(x),\\ \\sin(x),\\ \\cos(2x),\\ \\sin(2x),\\ \\ldots\n$$\n\nare orthogonal with respect to the inner product defined above.\n\nMore precisely, for integers $m,n\\ge 1,$ we have the following orthogonality relations on $[-\\pi,\\pi)$:\n\n- $\\langle \\cos(mx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0$ if $m\\ne n,$\n\n- $\\langle \\sin(mx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=0$ if $m\\ne n,$ and\n\n- $\\langle \\sin(mx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0$ for all $m,n.$\n\nAlso, the constant function $1$ is orthogonal to every sine and cosine:\n\n- $\\langle 1,\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0,$ and\n\n- $\\langle 1,\\sin(nx)\\rangle=0$ for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nFinally, we will also use the squared norms of these functions:\n\n$$\n\\langle 1,1\\rangle=2\\pi,\n\\quad\n\\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=\\langle \\sin(nx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=\\pi\n\\quad\n\\text{for all } n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nLet's see how this works in concrete examples.\n\n**Note:** Both intervals $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ and $[0,2\\pi)$ are considered standard in this context. We’ll use $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ in tutorials, but some exercises use $[0,2\\pi).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168036 #edb/ref 1065 14639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168036 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9c1c7f37-624c-5c49-9ded-2b3dd3b37d7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168036 #edb/ref 1067 "Reminder: Orthogonal Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168043 #edb/ref 1064 "Once again, recall that we are considering a $2\\pi$-periodic function $f(x)$ that is piecewise continuous on $[-\\pi,\\pi).$ We represent $f$ by its Fourier series\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\big).\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients, we use the inner product on $[-\\pi,\\pi)$:\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x), g(x)\\rangle=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe have already seen the derivation for the coefficient $a_0$ early in the lesson.\n\nNow, we derive the formula for $a_n$ for $n\\ge 1{:}$\n\n- First, we take the inner product of both sides with $\\cos(nx){:}$\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle\n=\n\\left\\langle \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_k\\cos(kx)+b_k\\sin(kx)\\big),\\,\\cos(nx)\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\n- Using linearity, we get\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle\n=\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}\\langle 1,\\cos(nx)\\rangle\n\\!+\\! \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} a_k\\langle \\cos(kx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle\n\\!+\\! \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} b_k\\langle \\sin(kx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle.\n$$\n\n- By orthogonality, $\\langle 1,\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0,$ $\\langle \\sin(kx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0$ for all $k,$ and $\\langle \\cos(kx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0$ whenever $k\\ne n,$ so\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=a_n\\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle.\n$$\n\nSince $\\displaystyle \\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} \\cos^2(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\pi,$ we obtain\n\n$$\na_n=\\dfrac{\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle}{\\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle}\n=\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\cos(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, we derive the formula for $b_n$ for $n\\ge 1{:}$\n\n- First, we take the inner product of both sides with $\\sin(nx){:}$\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle\n=\n\\left\\langle \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_k\\cos(kx)+b_k\\sin(kx)\\big),\\,\\sin(nx)\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\n- Using linearity, we get\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle\n=\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}\\langle 1,\\sin(nx)\\rangle\n\\!+\\! \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} a_k\\langle \\cos(kx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle\n\\!+\\! \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} b_k\\langle \\sin(kx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle.\n$$\n\n- By orthogonality, $\\langle 1,\\sin(nx)\\rangle=0,$ $\\langle \\cos(kx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=0$ for all $k,$ and $\\langle \\sin(kx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=0$ whenever $k\\ne n,$ so\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=b_n\\langle \\sin(nx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle.\n$$\n\nSince $\\displaystyle \\langle \\sin(nx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} \\sin^2(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\pi,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nb_n=\\dfrac{\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle}{\\langle \\sin(nx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle}\n=\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\sin(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nPutting these together, the Fourier coefficients are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\\,n\\ge 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\\,n\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168043 #edb/ref 1065 14668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168043 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "dc7b8526-b305-5026-8e7d-5e426bbe1a15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168043 #edb/ref 1067 "Deriving the Formulas For the Fourier Coefficients" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168052 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that a function $f(x)$ is *$p$-periodic* if $f(x+p)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}.$  \n\nNow, if $F(x)$ is defined on an interval $x \\in [a,b)$ of length $p,$ then its **$p$-periodic extension** $f$ is defined by the two properties:  \n\n- $f(x+p)=f(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x \\in [a,b).$\n\nFor example, let $F(x) = -x$ be defined on $x\\in [0,2).$ Now, suppose $f(x)$ is the $2$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,2)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$ The graphs of $y=F(x)$ and $y=f(x)$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/fe/fe66db8a73acee21bbd5f7c95d7598b448cb678408d2f47754ee4c30aefd5b14.png)\n\n**Watch out!** We should always be aware of the difference between $F(x)$ and its extension $f(x).$ For instance, consider the function $F(x)=2\\sin(x)$ defined on $x \\in [0,\\pi)$ and its $\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x).$ The graphs of $y=F(x)$ and $y=f(x)$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/b1/b1299bb593554a930a12870d39c74aa8d23ea97027b9e9e253e8fda6b592e42a.png)\n\nNotice that $f(x)$ is not just $2\\sin(x)!$ But it would coincide with $2\\sin(x)$ if we were to consider the $2\\pi$-periodic extension of $F(x)=2\\sin(x)$ from, say, $x \\in [0,2\\pi]$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168052 #edb/ref 1065 14853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168052 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bbf1ed42-199b-56dd-ba70-e35c3597339a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168052 #edb/ref 1067 "Periodic Extensions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168053 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's derive the formula for the coefficient $a_0$ by taking the inner product of the entire Fourier series \n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\big)\n$$\n\nwith the constant function $1.$\n\n- **Step 1.** Start with the Fourier series and take the inner product of both sides with $1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),1\\rangle\n=\n\\left\\langle \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\big),\\,1\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Use the linearity of the inner product to distribute it across the sum:\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),1\\rangle\n=\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}\\langle 1,1\\rangle\n+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a_n\\langle \\cos(nx),1\\rangle\n+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} b_n\\langle \\sin(nx),1\\rangle\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Apply the orthogonality property. For any $n\\ge 1,$ we have $\\langle \\cos(nx),1\\rangle=0$ and $\\langle \\sin(nx),1\\rangle=0.$ This makes all the terms in the sums equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),1\\rangle=\\dfrac{a_0}{2}\\langle 1,1\\rangle\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Solve for $a_0.$ We just need to calculate $\\langle 1,1\\rangle=\\displaystyle\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} 1\\cdot 1\\,\\textrm{d}x=2\\pi.$\n\n$$\na_0=\\dfrac{2\\langle f(x),1\\rangle}{\\langle 1,1\\rangle}\n=\\dfrac{2\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\cdot 1\\,\\textrm{d}x}{2\\pi}\n=\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nWe find the formulas for $a_n$ and $b_n$ (for $n\\ge 1$) in a similar way.\n\n- To find $a_n,$ we take the inner product of the Fourier series with $\\cos(nx).$ By orthogonality, the only term that is not zero on the right-hand side is the one involving $\\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle.$\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle = a_n \\langle \\cos(nx), \\cos(nx)\\rangle\n$$\n\nSince $\\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}\\cos^2(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\pi,$ we can solve for $a_n{:}$\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle}{\\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle}\n=\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\cos(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\n- Similarly, to find $b_n,$ we take the inner product with $\\sin(nx){:}$\n\n$$\nb_n = \\dfrac{\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle}{\\langle \\sin(nx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle}\n=\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\sin(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\n**Note:** If $f$ is the $2\\pi$-periodic extension of a function $F$ defined on $[-\\pi,\\pi),$ then we can replace $f(x)$ with $F(x)$ inside the integrals.\n\nNow that we have the formulas for all the coefficients, let's work through a concrete example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168053 #edb/ref 1065 14854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168053 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c5d2770f-53de-5939-9ec7-4155aef67173" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168053 #edb/ref 1067 "The Formula For the Constant Term" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311951 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186451213 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186311951 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186451214 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186311951 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186451215 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186311951 #edb/ref 1069 "q-338490" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311951 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311951 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $F(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- **II.** $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- **III.** $f(x)=f(x-2\\pi )$ for all $x\\in R$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186311951 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2b7e93bb-008a-52c4-ad5d-95180e45a411" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311951 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F,$ we have  \n\n- $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\pi $-periodicity: \n\n$$\nf(x+\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi )=f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)\\ne F(x)$ for $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =f((x-\\pi )+\\pi ) \\\\  & =f(x-\\pi ) \\\\  & =f((x-2\\pi )+\\pi ) \\\\  & =f(x-2\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311969 #edb/ref 1080 "\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311969 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311969 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "01360f92-7aa0-57d4-94ae-48f679e6d3c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311978 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311978 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311978 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "02879327-9074-58b7-a673-9af647dd76a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312018 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312018 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312018 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "067f1f01-946a-508e-8cd3-212922a30cb6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312040 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312040 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312040 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "08fed8a6-dde9-59a6-bc31-184c4876910c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312055 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218631205